nt who has a high enough GPS result  because they do have a high likelihood  of failing active surveillance because  they would have a biopsy upgrade they  may want to discuss do any immediate  therapy rather than wait the three years  whereas if you have a low GPS of 16 or  lower within three years there's a 75%  chance of not finding any higher grade  disease and so patients and physicians  can be more comfortable with the fact  that they'll likely be able to stay on  active surveillance for at least three  years and these GPS cut-offs actually  outperform the PI rad score one through  four vs pie-rats five and the authors  conclude again that it may be reasonable  to use GPS and shared decision-making to  determine who benefits from active  surveillance and that patients who have  a GPS of higher than 29 are more more  likely than not to fail active  surveillance in three years  so in review in terms of strengths  multi-gene signatures have been shown  schon underlying biology cannot be fully  assessed by phenotypic markers so this  includes clean path features as well as  imaging the the added strength of  genomic testing is also that it's  centralized and its objective in terms  of its measurement with minimal  variability and you can see those in the  analytic validation studies for each of  the assays  there's also a much lower threshold for  adoption and and implementation there's  there's no capital expenditure in terms  of getting an MRI the the equipment and  also training the the staff as well  there's also ever growing clinical  validation utility data coming out for  genomic tests in terms of limitations  there is limited data or how useful  these tests are an MRI targeted biopsy  setting however more data is to come in  the in the next few years on that and  there's no long-term outcomes and after  surveillance populations so well that  previous UCSF data that had just shown  was the first of its kind and then after  surveillance population it was only for  a couple of years and we need the  longer-term outcomes to see how well  genomic tests can help and risk stratify  these patients and then lastly there's  no their rather little health economic  data in terms of stand alone versus  combined use of MRI and genomics to see  what's most cost effective in terms of  combining both genomic testing and MRI  in low and intermediate risk patients  for practices that are already using MRI  target biopsies MRI really should be  sufficient in an inin by itself if a  patient has low pie-rats score so  specifically a PI reg 1 and 2 and they  have a 3 plus 3 on their biopsy based on  all the data that we just looked at as  well as a significant amount of data in  the literature these patients are not  likely harboring higher grade disease  now if a patient has a 3 plus 3 on  biopsy and they have a pie-rats 3 4 or 5  they would benefit from genomic testing  because again the positive predictive  for pi res 3 4 or 5 is not that great  and it's not entirely known whether or  not the patient actually had the the  highest grade actually sampled and the  same issue also exists for 3 plus 4  biopsies again there's a there's a  controversy on whether or not 3 plus 4  patients all have clinically significant  disease and there's a subset of these 3  plus 4 patients that do go on after  surveillance so simply finding 3 plus 4  on biopsy does not necessarily give you  enough information to make a treatment  decision between the immediate therapy  and after surveillance and for practices  who are only using the 12 course  systematic biopsy it becomes highly  context dependent on which methodology  to use I would offer that genomic tests  typically have a lot of patient  assistance programs to reduce financial  talks to see for patients and they also  present with minimal logistical  challenges for the patient they would  already had the biopsy and the biopsy  would be available at the pathology  office so there's no other additional  visits or interaction that would need to  take place to get a genomic test done  for those patients so in terms of future  directions we still need larger studies  to clarify what the full range of  genomic test results are for each  pie-rats group or parrot scoring group  and then we'd also like to have  confirmatory studies showing that GPS  results as well as other genomic test  results are still informative in the MRI  targeted biopsy context and specifically  improving area under the curves results  there'd also be a need for health  economic analysis to determine what  where the cost and benefit is for either  using one modality or the other alone or  in combination and then lastly whether  or not MRI and genomic tests can be used  to reduce or increase surveillance  intensity so there is a large push  towards reducing the number of  unnecessary biopsies and MRI data as  well as genomic testing data could  potentially help us tailor a module  the intensity of surveillance for  patient so that we can minimize biopsies  and the attendant side-effects so thank  you very much for attending this talk I  appreciate your time and if you have any  questions feel free to contact me thank  you


